{
  "id": "find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array",
  "questionId": "find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array",
  "slug": "find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array",
  "title": "Find First and Last Position of Element in Sorted Array",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "a plain binary search finds 'a' matching index, but with duplicates that could land anywhere in the run of equal values - how could you push the search toward one end?",
    "run binary search for the leftmost occurrence: whenever nums[mid] == target, don't stop, instead keep searching the left half to see if an earlier match exists",
    "run a second, separate binary search for the rightmost occurrence, this time continuing into the right half whenever nums[mid] == target",
    "if the leftmost search never finds target at all, you can immediately return [-1, -1] without needing the second search"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "stopping at the first index where nums[mid] == target instead of continuing to narrow toward the boundary, which can return a middle occurrence instead of the true first or last",
    "not handling the empty array case before starting the binary search, causing an out-of-bounds midpoint calculation",
    "using one binary search and trying to expand outward linearly from the found index to find the boundaries, which degrades to O(n) when there are many duplicates",
    "off-by-one errors in the low/high bounds update that cause an infinite loop or skip the correct boundary index"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of integers sorted in ascending order, which may\ncontain duplicate values, and a target value.\n\n**Find the first index and the last index at which the target appears, and return them as a two-element array `[firstIndex, lastIndex]`, or `[-1, -1]` if the target doesn't appear at all.**\n\nYour solution must run in O(log n) time, so scanning the array linearly isn't acceptable.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= length of nums <= 10^5",
      "array is sorted in non-decreasing order",
      "nums may contain duplicate values"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty array",
      "target appears exactly once",
      "every element in the array equals target",
      "target is smaller than every element or larger than every element"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
